{
  "configuration_id": "config_001",
  "reason": "With no prior experiments, the most critical uncertainty is whether combining RNA and image modalities with strong learned encoders (scGPT for RNA, ViT-L for image) in a multimodal setting yields a meaningful accuracy gain over what single-modality or weaker-encoder approaches could achieve. Config_001 (multimodal, singleprofile, scgpt, imagenet_vit_l, concat) tests this core hypothesis in the simplest structural form—no aggregation layer, single profile—so any performance signal is cleanly attributable to the encoder quality and modality combination rather than confounded by aggregation or multiprofile complexity. If it performs well, subsequent experiments can exploit by adding multiprofile + transformer/avgpool aggregation; if it underperforms, I can ablate to identify whether the RNA encoder, image encoder, or fusion strategy is the bottleneck. Cost: moderate-to-high (two large pretrained encoders: scGPT and ViT-L), but no aggregation overhead, making it roughly 60-70% of the cost of the most expensive multiprofile+transformer configs. This is the best use of experiment 1 because it establishes a strong anchor point and isolates the single most impactful design question (multimodal + strong encoders) before spending budget on finer-grained ablations."
}

